  "notes": [
    "The pricebook row is a per-megapixel figure, but neither model bills pixels times that rate. The estimator turns it into a price per output token: round-half-even(200958 x 1048576 / 1e6) = 210720 micro-USD buys the 7024 tokens of a 1024x1024 image at max, which is 30 micro-USD per token, OpenAI's 30 USD per 1M image output tokens.",
    "Output tokens follow OpenAI's calculator for GPT-Image-2.6: ceil(patches x round-half-even(patches x short / long) x (2000000 + width x height) / 4000000), times 4.2 at low and 3.3 at medium, rounded half up. MachGen's integer form, floor((tokens x tenths + 5) / 10), gave the same count as OpenAI's Math.round at every one of the 29146 valid sizes and five qualities (145730 cases).",
    "An I2I request adds 7024 tokens for each source image (priceIncludesInputImages is false): 1.048576 megapixels at the pricebook rate, whatever the image's own size. T2I adds none.",
    "The 30 percent discount runs to 2027-03-01T16:00:00Z. The estimator applies it to the total and rounds half up; because the list total is always 30 micro-USD times a whole token count, the discounted total is exactly 21 micro-USD per token.",
    "generation_credit_percent is 0 on both rows of the account pricebook, so no Generation Credit is expected on these tasks.",
    "The seven probes show that both models answer this key on T2I and I2I (a 400, not a 403), that image_output.quality is validated before the size and takes exactly auto, low, medium, high, xhigh and max, and that an unknown key inside image_output is not refused at that stage.",
    "The two model pages print only the per-megapixel headline (0.201 USD, 0.141 USD discounted). The billing rule is on the REST guide, which covers both models, and in the estimator, which both contract entries share."
  ]
